Subject: [S] Summary: Aggregating while merging factor levels.
Message-ID: <85256878.00697E72.00@mail.spgdag.com>

Hello all,

I got three replies to my query about aggregating continuous variables while
merging levels of factor variables.  Thanks to Vadim Ogranovich, Bill Dunlap and
Brian Ripley for their insights.  You will find attached the original question
followed by their replies.

Thanks again to all respondents,

Gérald Jean
Analyste-conseil (statistiques), Actuariat
télephone            : (418) 835-8839
télecopieur          : (418) 835-5865
courrier électronique: gerald.jean at spgdag.ca

"In God we trust all others must bring data"

---------------

On Mon, 31 Jan 2000, [iso-8859-1] "G?rald Jean" wrote:

> if we merge the levels of one, or more, factors in a data.frame the resulting
> data.frame will have several identical factor combinations.  Is it possible to
> aggregate (summing is good enough in my case) the cells corresponding to
> identical factor combinations.  For exemple take the builtin dataset "claims",
> claims has three factors and two continuous variables.  Suppose I want to
merge
> levels "17-20" with "21-24" and "30-34" with "35-39" of factor age, and I also
> want to merge levels "A" with "B" and "C" with "D" of factor type.  While, or
> after, doing so I would like to have the continuous variables "cost" and
> "number" summed for identical factor combinations which means that the dataset
> will shrink row wise?  Can this be done efficiently?  I know that merge.levels
> will merge factor levels but that is all it will do?  A solution involving the
> builtin function "aggregate" will not work for me, the datasets are too big.

1) Bill Dunlap's solution:

Would merge.levels following by agg.sum() (which may replace aggregate
when FUN=sum) be quick enough:

agg.sum <- function(x, by, sort.it = T) {
        if(!is.data.frame(by))
                by <- data.frame(by)
        if(sort.it) {
                ord <- do.call("order", unname(by))
                x <- x[ord]
                by <- by[ord,  ]
        }
        logical.diff <- function(group)
        group[-1] != group[ - length(group)]
        change <- logical.diff(by[[1]])
        for(i in seq(along = by)[-1])
                change <- change | logical.diff(by[[i]])
        by <- by[c(T, change),  , drop = F]
        by$x <- diff(c(0, cumsum(x)[c(change, T)]))
        by
}
unname <- function(x) {
        names(x) <- NULL
        x
}

2) Brian Ripley's solution

library nnet has a C function which is used exactly for this purpose in
multinom. Given numerical matrices X and Y is sums rows of Y corresponding
to identical rows of X.  A data frame does map to a numerical matrix
(with factors mapping to numerical codes).

You would need to do a bit of work to interface to it, but it is fast.

3) Vadim Ogranovich's suggestion:

Look at tapply(), e.g. tapply(x, list(age, type), "sum")

