Subject: [S] Factor interactions in glm's
Message-ID: <8525687A.005E8F49.00@mail.spgdag.com>

Dear S-users,

when we fit a glm where the independent variables are factors and the contrasts
option for factor is set to contr.treatment we expect, and actually get, a
coefficient estimate for each level of each factor excepted for the reference
level and this is fine.

To make my question easier to formulate suppose we have 2 factors, A and B, with
three levels each as follows:

> levels(ttt$A)
[1] "low"    "medium" "high"
> levels(ttt$B)
[1] "yes"   "no"    "maybe"

For A I will have a coefficient for "medium" and one for "high", similarly for B
 I'll get a coefficient for "no" and one for "maybe".  Now
when I put the interaction A:B in the model I get coefficients for the main
effects as before and I also get coefficients for:
A medium : B no, A medium : B maybe, A high : B no, A high : B maybe.  Should'nt
 I also get coefficients for the crosses of the
reference levels with the levels, excepted the reference, of the other factor as
 well? i.e.
A low : B no, A low : B maybe, A medium : B yes and A high : B yes?  I know that
 this little exemple would yield only nine cells and
that we could'nt estimate 12 coefficients.  But assume we have many other
factors and the number of observations is not a problem.

Any insights?

