> To make my question easier to formulate suppose we have 2 factors, A and B, 
with
> three levels each as follows:
> 
> > levels(ttt$A)
> [1] "low"    "medium" "high"
> > levels(ttt$B)
> [1] "yes"   "no"    "maybe"
> 
> For A I will have a coefficient for "medium" and one for "high", similarly 
> for 
B
>  I'll get a coefficient for "no" and one for "maybe".  Now
> when I put the interaction A:B in the model I get coefficients for the main
> effects as before and I also get coefficients for:
> A medium : B no, A medium : B maybe, A high : B no, A high : B maybe.  
Should'nt
>  I also get coefficients for the crosses of the
> reference levels with the levels, excepted the reference, of the other factor 
as
>  well? i.e.
> A low : B no, A low : B maybe, A medium : B yes and A high : B yes? 

reference A cross B is the main effect of B.

Think of it this way. You want a linear predictor for each of 9 a-b 
combinations. This is done by

eta_ab = mu + alpha_a + beta_b + gamma_ab

and for identifiability alpha_low = beta_yes = 0, _and_ gamma_ab is
zero whenever a=low or b=yes.

