Message-ID: <389BE98D.DCAA36BD@candiensten.nl>

Alfred,

I once had this problem with a bunch of text files. One solution could
be:

Put all your files in one directory, use the function files.in.dir which
gives you a vector of the file names.

Use a loop to call the import.data function, something like:

test <- files.in.dir("C:\\temp")
for (i in length(test)){
        filename <- paste("C:\\temp\\",", test[i], sep="")
        dfname <- paste("D",i,sep="")
        import.data(DataFrame=dfname,File=filename,FileType="EXCEL")
}

You will get data frames called D1, D2, etc.
And use cbind to combine all the data frames

zz <- paste("D",1:length(test),sep="")
tmp <- as.name(paste(zz,collapse=","))
result <- eval(parse(text=paste("cbind(",tmp,")")))

Your problem with the tab names is more difficult, I don't see a quick
and easy solution to that. There is no easy way to get the tab name of
an excel sheet with an S-PLUS function. Is there?

What you could do if you really need to automate it I think, is to use
automation. See page 515 of the programmer's guide where you can get a
specific property of an excel workboot via automation.

Hope this helps,
Longhow

