On Sun, 6 Feb 2000 msvika at mscc.huji.ac.il wrote:

> 
> Dear S-plus users!
> First of all, a lot of thanks to all of you for your quick replies and
> assistance. I've got a lot of replies from Brian Ripley, Matt
> Calder, Bill Venables, Chuck Taylor and others and now my C code runs
> fine!
> 
> My current effort is to include S functions into C code.
> Does anybody have an experience with such procedure, i.e. I would like to
> call S function "pnorm" in my C code? Does it enough efficient?

There are several possible ideas here:

1) All versions.  To use call_S to call the S function from your C code.
2) S-PLUS 5.x.    To use .Call to run your C code, and eval() with that
   to run bits of S.
3) To call the entry point in the S engine that S itself calls.

1) and 2) are hard work, and 1) in particular is slow.  Unfortunately
the entry point is not publicly documented, but it has been used by addons.
I think that its name differs by version, too.  So other addons have
used their own pnorm: locfit has

#define ROOT_2          1.4142135623730950488016887242096980785697
double pnorm(x,mu,s)
double x, mu, s;
{ if(x == mu)
    return(0.5);
  x = (x-mu)/s;
  if(x > 0) return((1 + erf(x/ROOT_2))/2);
  return(erfc(-x/ROOT_2)/2);
}

which is OK if you have erf and erfc (but Windows compilers tend not to).
Survival5 uses similar ideas.

