From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Sun Feb  6 08:27:36 2000
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Sun, 06 Feb 2000 08:27:36 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] lsfit or glm: specifying an intercept
In-Reply-To: <4.1.20000205124241.00b969c0@uclink4.berkeley.edu>
References: <4.1.20000205124241.00b969c0@uclink4.berkeley.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10002060814520.29435-100000@auk.stats>

On Sat, 5 Feb 2000, Timothy J. Wade wrote:

> sorry if this question has been asked. I've only just subscribed.
> 
> I would like to fit a simple least squares regression using lsfit or glm,
> but I want to force the least squares regression line and equation through
> a specified intercept point (y=1).
> 
> This is simple to do in excel, but I can't find the command to accomplish
> this through lsfit or glm.

lsfit:

lsfit(x, y - 1, intercept=F)

lm:

lm(y - 1 ~ x - 1)

glm:

glm(y - 1 ~ x - 1)  or glm(y ~ x - 1 + offset(rep(1, length(x))) )

The last one is the only one that will predict y rather than y-1.

It is more complicated in S, but then this notation is routinely used to do
things people would not dream of doing in Excel.

-- 
