From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Mon Feb  7 23:16:18 2000
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Mon, 07 Feb 2000 23:16:18 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] error trapping question
In-Reply-To: <389F4EF0.8607AB35@pdf.com>
References: <389F4EF0.8607AB35@pdf.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10002072307490.23938-100000@auk.stats>

On Mon, 7 Feb 2000, Paul Lin wrote:

> Does anyone know how to do error trapping in S-plus?
> I've dodged this issue thus far by using other characteristics
> in the data in the if(condition) to determine when the S-plus
> commands will bomb.  But it's tedious and cumbersome!
> 
> Specifically, say that I am running a series of lm() in a loop:
> 
>       for (i in index) { lm(formula, data for each loop i) }
> 
> And I know that some of the lm()'s will bomb - due to collinearity,
> obs dropped, and so forth.  Is there a way for me to check whether
> the lm() will run before I run it?  Something like:
> 
>       for (i in index) {
>               if (lm(formula, data for each loop i) returns no error) {
>                       lm(formula, data for each loop i)
>               }
>       }
> 
> Thanks in advance!

The try() idea I mentioned in a post earlier today (a wrapper for restart)
is precisely what you want here.  You can also use options(error=)
to install your own error handler, but I would stick to the simplest
possible idea.

try() is in S-PLUS 2000 and 5.1. A simpler version (from V&R's forthcoming
S Programming) is

try <- function(expr, first = T)
{
  restart(first)
  if(first) { 
    first <- F
    expr
  } else NULL
}

Call this with

      for (i in index) {
         res <- try(lm(formula, data for i))
         if(!is.null(res))  process the result
      }

You will get to see error messages unless you do install an error
handler, but all the cases will be run.

