From lancelot at telecomplus.sn  Tue Feb  8 09:11:03 2000
From: lancelot at telecomplus.sn (CIRAD EMVT)
Date: Tue, 08 Feb 2000 09:11:03 +0000
Subject: [S] Summary: 1-M matched case-control
Message-ID: <389FDDA7.A268E981@telecomplus.sn>

Dear all,

Last week, I posted the following request:

>I would like to estimate odds ratios from a 1-M matched case-control
>design (1 case and M controls). I know how to do when M=1, and I know
>that I should use coxph when M > 1, but I don't know how to implement
>it. I would highly appreciate any help, preferably with a small example.
>
>I am running S+ 2000 r2.

Thanks to Brad Biggerstaff, Murray Finkelstein and Terry Therneau for
their quick, helpful and efficient solutions, which are basically the
same: use coxph with a particular coding for the response. Below are
their replies and a test on a dataset provided by Hosmer & Lemeshow in
their book:
Hosmer, D. W. and S. Lemeshow (1989). Applied logistic regression.
New-York, John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
chapter 7, & 7.5, results p. 203, dataset Appendix 4 p. 266-269.

### 1. Brad Biggerstaff
Below is a wrapper function I wrote to coxph to do matched logistic
regression. You need to specify the matching variable as a variable in
the dataframe using quotes. For example, for some mystery dataset
looking at the effect of smoking on height (Tall=0:1), matched on a
variable Age, a call might be:
mlogitreg(Tall~Smoke,height.data,matching.variable="Age")

Note:  With the new version of coxph, I needed to add a parameter
toler.chol; take it out if you're on an older version that doesn't have
that parameter.

"mlogitreg"<-
function(formula = formula(data), data = sys.parent(), weights, subset =
T, na.action, matching.variable, eps = 0.0001, toler.chol=1e-9, iter.max
= 10, x = F, y = T)
{
if(missing(matching.variable))
  stop("matching.variable must be specified")
call <- match.call()
m <- match.call()
m$matching.variable <- m$eps <- m$toler.chol <- m$iter.max <- m$x <- m$y
<- NULL     
m[[1]] <- as.name("model.frame")
m <- eval(m, sys.parent())
Terms <- attr(m, "terms")
CaseStatus <- model.extract(m, "response")
weights <- model.extract(m, "weights")
offset <- attr(Terms, "offset")
if(length(unique(CaseStatus)) != 2)
  stop(" Response must have only two levels")
if(is.factor(CaseStatus)) {
  CaseStatus <- codes(CaseStatus)
  CaseStatus[CaseStatus == 2] <- 0
  }
if(options()$contrasts[1] != "contr.treatment") {
  options(contrasts = c("contr.treatment", "contr.poly"))
  cat("\n ...factor contrasts reset to contr.treatment\n\n")
  }
X <- model.matrix(Terms, m)
PseudoTime <- rep(1, length(CaseStatus))
PseudoSurv <- Surv(PseudoTime, CaseStatus)
if(is.data.frame(data)) {
  if(!is.character(matching.variable[[1]]))
    MatchingVar <- data[subset, as.character(matching.variable[[1]])]
  else MatchingVar <- data[subset, matching.variable[[1]]]
  }
else MatchingVar <- eval(as.name(matching.variable[[1]]))[subset]
fit <- coxph.fit(X, PseudoSurv, strata = strata(MatchingVar), offset =
offset, weights = weights, method = "exact", eps = eps, iter.max =
iter.max, rownames = row.names(m), toler.chol = toler.chol)
# get rid of Intercept
fit$coefficients <- fit$coefficients[-1]
#get rid of Intercept row and column
fit$var <- fit$var[-1, -1]
attr(fit, "class") <- "coxph"
fit$n <- length(CaseStatus)
fit$call <- call
if(x) fit$x <- X
if(y) fit$y <- CaseStatus
fit
}

### 2. Murray Finkelstein
Assign each case and matched controls to an individual stratum, then run
stratified Cox regression.
Code failure time to be 1 for all subjects. Cases have outcome 1,
controls outcome 0.

### 3. Terry Therneau
1. Make each case-control set a strata
2. Set the time variable to 1 (or any other constant you like)
3. Status: 0=control, 1=case
coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ x1 + x2 +.....+ strata(casegroup), data=....)
4. If there is more than 1 case per group (M:N matching), then you need
to also say "method=exact", but for M:1 matching all of the methods for
ties give the same answer.
5. No preprocessing of the covariates is necessary.

######################################### Test
> ## H&L p.203, table 7.8
> #        coef   s.e.
> # smoke  0.554  0.488
> # ht     0.098  1.395
> # ui     0.525  0.549
> # ptd    1.532  0.638
> # lwt   -0.005  0.009
> 
> ## Therneau
> mod1 <- coxph(Surv(time=rep(1,length(low)), event=low) ~ smoke + ht + ui + 
> ptd + lwt + strata(str), data=M13lbw)
> round(cbind(B=coef(mod1), "SE(B)"=sqrt(diag(mod1$var)), 
> t=coef(mod1)/sqrt(diag(mod1$var))),3)
           B SE(B)      t 
smoke  0.554 0.489  1.132
   ht  0.098 1.395  0.070
   ui  0.525 0.549  0.957
  ptd  1.533 0.638  2.402
  lwt -0.005 0.009 -0.599
> 
> ## Biggerstaff
> mod2 <- mlogitreg(low ~ smoke + ht + ui + ptd + lwt, data=M13lbw, 
> matching.variable="str")
> round(cbind(B=coef(mod2), "SE(B)"=sqrt(diag(mod2$var)), 
> t=coef(mod2)/sqrt(diag(mod2$var))),3)
           B SE(B)      t 
smoke  0.554 0.489  1.132
   ht  0.098 1.395  0.070
   ui  0.525 0.549  0.957
  ptd  1.533 0.638  2.402
  lwt -0.005 0.009 -0.599

################################################

Many thanks again !

Renaud

