References: <200002061903.UAA04458@mail.boku.ac.at>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.21.0002081121380.23245-101000@newton.gbar.dtu.dk>

I think it is the outliers in the beginning (or end) of the series.  It
is not due to acf() doing it's calculations in single precision (I have
tested this on an example).

I have attached a postscript file containing one page of something I once
wrote about using the 'usual' estimator of correlation (eq. 26) instead of
SACF (eq. 25).  The denominator of (25) will include all the outliers, but
the denominator of (26) will contain all outliers in the leftmost
square-root and will skip the first |k| in the rightmost 
square-root.  Since the numerators are identical in (25) and (26), 
outliers in the beginning (or end) of the series will cause the
absolute values of the results from (26) to be larger than those from (25).  

Regards,
Henrik

