>>>>> "BR" == Prof Brian D Ripley <ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk> writes:

  BR> On Mon, 7 Feb 2000, Paul Lin wrote:

  >> Does anyone know how to do error trapping in S-plus? I've dodged this
  >> issue thus far by using other characteristics in the data in the
  >> if(condition) to determine when the S-plus commands will bomb. But it's
  >> tedious and cumbersome!
  >> 
  >> Specifically, say that I am running a series of lm() in a loop:
  >> 
  >> for (i in index) { lm(formula, data for each loop i) }
  >> 
  >> And I know that some of the lm()'s will bomb - due to collinearity, obs
  >> dropped, and so forth. Is there a way for me to check whether the lm()
  >> will run before I run it? Something like:
  >> 
  >> for (i in index) { if (lm(formula, data for each loop i) returns no
  >> error) { lm(formula, data for each loop i) } }
  >> 
  >> Thanks in advance!

  BR> The try() idea I mentioned in a post earlier today (a wrapper for
  BR> restart) is precisely what you want here. You can also use
  BR> options(error=) to install your own error handler, but I would stick to
  BR> the simplest possible idea.

  BR> try() is in S-PLUS 2000 and 5.1. A simpler version (from V&R's
  BR> forthcoming S Programming) is

  BR> try <- function(expr, first = T) { restart(first) if(first) { first <- F
  BR> expr } else NULL }

  BR> Call this with

  BR>       for (i in index) { res <- try(lm(formula, data for i))
  BR> if(!is.null(res)) process the result }

  BR> You will get to see error messages unless you do install an error
  BR> handler, but all the cases will be run.

  BR> -- Brian D. Ripley, ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk Professor of Applied
  BR> Statistics, http://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~ripley/ University of Oxford,
  BR> Tel: +44 1865 272861 (self) 1 South Parks Road, +44 1865 272860 (secr)
  BR> Oxford OX1 3TG, UK Fax: +44 1865 272595

