From gardar at stat.ohio-state.edu  Tue Feb  8 21:22:57 2000
From: gardar at stat.ohio-state.edu (Gardar Johannesson)
Date: Tue, 08 Feb 2000 16:22:57 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary for: from SAS to nlme 3.1
Message-ID: <Pine.SOL.4.10.10002081608430.23596-100000@spatial.mps.ohio-state.edu>

The s-news posting was:

I have been trying to figure out how to fit the following PROC MIXED
model in SAS,
   class site date;
   model log_con=;
   random site date;
using lme() in Splus 2000 (release 2) -- and I'm getting frustrated...

That is, I want to fit
Y(s,d) = mu + A(s) + B(d) + e(s,d)
with A(s) iid N(0,s(A)^2), B(d) iid N(0,s(B)^2) and e(s,d) iid
N(0,s(e)^2).

#####

The answer is; use varcomp() -- lme() is at it's best for grouped
data. That is,
> is.random(mydata$site) <- T
> is.random(mydata$time) <- T
> fit <- varcomp(log.con ~ 1 + site + time, data=mydata, method='reml')

A little more on the data.  The data is spatial-temporal data.  There are
monitors located at different sites (the spatial dependence) generating a
single observation every X days (temporal dependence). The idea is to fit
a simple model with:

(1) some correlation between observations taken at different
time-points from the same monitor (one monitor at each site) -- i.e.,
temporal correlation.
(2) some correlation between observations taken at the same (or close by)
time-point at different sites -- spatial correlation.

Any ideas are wellcome... using tools available in Splus.

The model fitted by varcomp() above gives only (1) fixed correlation
between observation taken at different time-points from the same site and
(2) a fixed spatial correlation between observations taken at different
sites at the same time-point.  Which is a good start, but an
auto-correlation plot (using acf()) of the observations taken from a
single site look more like a AR(1) pattern than just a fixed (horizontal)
line (as given by the varcomp() model).

I want to thank Renaud Lancelot, Jose' Pinheiro, James H. Roger for
quick replies.

Renaud Lancelot pointed to a document (by Bates and Pineiro) at
http://franz.stat.wisc.edu/pub/NLME/
on the difference between proc mixed (in SAS) and lme().

Thanks,
Gardar

