From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Wed Feb  9 10:17:01 2000
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Wed, 09 Feb 2000 10:17:01 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] a question on the "lapply" function
In-Reply-To: <006f01bf72e2$81af54f0$c9ada19d@ch.genedata.com>
References: <006f01bf72e2$81af54f0$c9ada19d@ch.genedata.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10002091009270.28363-100000@auk.stats>

On Wed, 9 Feb 2000, Claire Pallez wrote:

> 
> Hi all,
> 
> I am new on this list and I am a new Splus user.
>  I use Splus 2000.
> I am currently reading Splus algorithm and I can't find what this expression
> meens :
> lapply ( x , "[" , ... ).
> 

x is a list, and this means apply the subset operator to each element
of the list. Here is an example:

x <- list(a=1:10, b=11:20)
lapply(x, "[", 6:8)
$a:
[1] 6 7 8

$b:
[1] 16 17 18

so this has done list(a=a[6:8], b=b[6:8]).

This works because you can use "[" like

> "["(1:10, 6:8)
[1] 6 7 8

Finally a warning: as I understand it, this will always apply the
default subscript operator whatever class the objects in the list have.
See the BUGS section on the help page (at least in S-PLUS 5.1).
If that matters, write this as

lapply(x, function(z, i) z[i], 6:8)

