From archer-4 at medctr.osu.edu  Wed Feb  9 13:37:19 2000
From: archer-4 at medctr.osu.edu (Kellie Archer)
Date: Wed, 09 Feb 2000 08:37:19 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary, breaking y-axis
Message-ID: <s8a1276a.010@140.254.139.64>

Many thanks to Nicholas Barrowman, Robert Canales, Bert Gunter, Samuel Buttrey, 
Tim Hesterberg, and Amy Ferketich who responded to my original posting
' I have a dataset with one very extreme y-value, and I would like to produce a 
2-dimensional scatterplot with the y-axis broken between the bulk of the data 
and this one outlying value.  Any suggestions?'

Bert Gunter responded:
This is a matter of taste, of course. I do not like axis breaks, as I feel
that they often mislead  (Bill Cleveland has similar cautionary notes in his
writings). My preference is to produce a little arrow at the top (or bottom
if it's a low outlier) of the plot at the appropriate x-coordinate with the
(text) y value next to it. This is trivial in S-Plus.

Amy Ferketich pointed out that once a graph is created, you can convert it to 
an object, then select the y-axis and specify axis breaks.

Tim Hesterberg responded
* Create a copy of the data, with the single y value changed to a value
closer to the rest of the data; where you'd like it on the plot.

* Plot, using axes=F.  Add x axis and box using
        plot(..., axes=F)
        box()
        axis(side=1)

* Figure out where you want tick marks on the y axis, and how you want
them labeled.  You may find pretty(y[y != max(y)]) useful for the
bulk of the marks.

* Add the y axis using
        axis(side=2, at=(somewhere), labels=(how you want them labeled))

* Add a zig-zag line or pair of lines across the plot to indicate
the break, using lines(), segments(), or polygon().

Samuel Buttrey responded:
"Break.at" is any number between the bulk of the data
and the "huge" y-values; "jump" controls the amount of space between the
largest non-huge y and the huge ones. (All "huge" y's get plotted at the
same y-value.) The tricky part is drawing the y-axis. 

