Subject: [S] Summary: Weighted Nonlinear Least-Squares
Message-ID: <4.2.2.20000210093423.00aa0c90@mail.bea.nmfs.gov>

I had asked how to do nonlinear least-squares with row weighting; nls() 
does not include a "weights=" option.  I mentioned that the work-around 
given in MASS, while used by me gratefully, involved a chain reaction of 
work-arounds in subsequent analyses.

I received one answer, highly informative, from Jose' Pinheiro of Bell 
Labs.  I thought it had appeared on the S-News list, but as I've received 
two inquiries wondering about answers, here it is:

>>  The gnls function in the NLME library (http://nlme.stat.wisc.edu) 
allows you to fit nls models with weights (and also correlation 
structures). Even though the name of the argument is "weights", its

use is different from lm, for example. It allows you to specify a variance 
function which, in turn, will produce weights (this is documented in the 
software). If the weights are fixed, the estimation

method is equivalent to a weighted nonlinear least-squares, but if you want 
the weights to depend on parameters, or on the fitted values, for example, 
then the estimation will not be the same as nonlinear LS

(maximum likelihood is used, instead). Version 3.1 of NLME is included with 
S-PLUS 2000 (Windows). The latest version, 3.2, can be obtained from the 
URL above.  <<

Thanks again to Jose' Pinheiro for the help!

