From Luciano.Molinari at kispi.unizh.ch  Fri Feb 11 13:57:07 2000
From: Luciano.Molinari at kispi.unizh.ch (Molinari, Luciano)
Date: Fri, 11 Feb 2000 14:57:07 +0100
Subject: [S] survReg and censorReg
Message-ID: <136E7D899707D311A8290008C7BF17A413A201@obelix.kispi.unizh.ch>

I am working with S+2000 Rel.2 under WindowNT4.0.
Recently I was confronted with survival data, went over S+2000 Guide to
Statistics Vol.2  Ch.8-13, and now I have quite a few remarks (1:4  below,
perhaps also of interest to the casual user) and  questions (5:9, this
represent serious problems for the serious users, and should, I think, be
looked at also by MathSoft gurus)

Starting with the rather trivial, but still disturbing:
1) According to the above manual, p.224 and 225 "... NAs are appropriately
inserted ..." to make x, residuals and fit of the same length. This a great
idea. However, example cut and pasted from the manual, p.225:
---------------------
S+>fit <- coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ age + sex +
+  ph.ecog + ph.karno, data = lung,
+  na.action =na.omit)
S+>plot(lung$age, residuals(fit))
Error in plot: Lengths of x and y must match
--------------------------
Am I missing something?
Well, just while writing this message, I realised that  using the option
"na.action=na.exclude" fixes the problem.

2)
Anova.survReg expects its argument object to have a family component.
survReg objects, however, do not have such a component. This just messes up
some part of the printout.
Here also I just find the "solution": use censor and censorReg instead of
Surv ans survReg.

3) The possible values for parameter type in residuals.censorReg, as
indicated in the Help file, are not consistent with the header of the
function:
str(residuals.censorReg)
function(object, type = c("standardized", "deviance", "pearson", "working",
"response"))

4) The help file of "censor" is not consisten with its code (e.g., regarding
the parameter "type")
======================================================
5) Plot functions related to censorReg
------------------------------
S+>zzmp_censorReg(formula = censor(cdat, cens) ~ sex, data =
zzd,dist="gaus") 
S+>zzmp
Call:
censorReg(formula = censor(cdat, cens) ~ sex, data = zzd, distribution =
"gaus")
Distribution: Gaussian 
Coefficients:
 (Intercept)        sex 
   0.2278586 -0.0735974
Dispersion (scale) = 0.7703553
Log-likelihood: -320.8091 
Observations: 311 Total; 71 Censored
Parameters Estimated: 3 

Above is a correct censorReg object!
S+>plot(zzmp,ask=T)
Make a plot selection (or 0 to exit):
1: plot: All
2: plot: Residuals vs Fitted Values
3: plot: Sqrt of abs(Residuals) vs Fitted Values
4: plot: Response vs Fitted Values
5: plot: Probability Plot of Residuals
6: plot: Probability Plot of Fit
7: plot: "Stress" Plot
8: plot: Probability Plot of Fit for 6 Distributions
Selection: 5
WARNING: Point out of bounds: x = -4.288321, y = -2.946354
WARNING: Point out of bounds: x = -2.969978, y = -2.588257
....

This continues for a few hundred lines, depending on the size of the censor
object.
I found the source of the problem within probplot.censor where I omitted the
line (about line 25)
------------
if(xlim[1] <= 0 && Distribution != "Normal") xlim[1] <- 0.001
---------------
Alternatively one could write
-------------------------
if(xlim[1] <= 0 && distribution != "gaussian") xlim[1] <- 0.001
---------------
Both work for me, but ...

6) Plot functions related to censorReg. Here probplot.censorReg.
First of all with the same data as above:
---------------------------------
S+>probplot.censorReg(zzmp)
Error in censorReg.make.Y: Argument "link" is missing, with no default:
censorReg.make.Y(Y, Ter
ms, xfit$threshold)
-----------------------------------
Replace in the header of censorReg.make.Y
--------
function(Y, Terms, threshold, weights = rep(1, nrow(Y)))
---------------
function(Y, Terms, threshold, weights = rep(1, nrow(Y)), link = "id")
-----------------
Hope it works in general.

7) Then as in 5) above. Here my patch in probplot.censorReg, was by omitting
the line (which is wrong anyway):
---------------------
if(xlim[1] <= 0 && (distribution2 != "normal" | distribution2 !="gaussian"))
xlim[1] <- 0.001
-----------------
Alternatively one could replace the above line by:
-------
if(xlim[1] <= 0 && (distribution2 != "Normal" && distribution2 !=
"Gaussian")) xlim[1] <- 0.001
--------------------
Again it works for me, but ...

8) Plot functions related to censorReg.
-----------------
S+>plot(zzmp,ask=T)
Make a plot selection (or 0 to exit):
1: plot: All
2: plot: Residuals vs Fitted Values
3: plot: Sqrt of abs(Residuals) vs Fitted Values
4: plot: Response vs Fitted Values
5: plot: Probability Plot of Residuals
6: plot: Probability Plot of Fit
7: plot: "Stress" Plot
8: plot: Probability Plot of Fit for 6 Distributions
Selection: 8
Error in censorReg.make.Y(Y, Terms, threshold, casewt, li..: after adjusting
for argument thres
hold, some event times are less than zero
-----------------------------
The problem here is in probplot6.censorReg.
I have a private patch here, but it is too much ad hoc, to make it public.
======================================
9) Here I guess I need help.
I have been fighting against predict.censorReg for almost a couple of hours.
S+>zz2_censor(zz1$D[,1],zz1$D[,1]<log(60))
S+>zz3_censorReg(zz2~zz1$D[,3]+zz1$D[,3]^2,dist="gaus")
-------
This is a legitimate censorReg object.
S+>predict(zz3,p=c(.03,.10,.25,.5,.75,.90,.97),conf=F,se=F)
OK. It does not work anymore however, once the original data have been
deleted. I can understand this, predict.gam shows a similar behaviour.

Following gave me much more pain, and still I do not quite understand, while
eventually my solution worked.
S+>predict(zz3,p=c(.03,.10,.25,.5,.75,.90,.97),newdata=data.frame(seq(5,10,.
1)),conf=F,se=F)
Error in qftdist.dist(fit, X, data = data): argument X contains a variable
name that is not con
tained in the data used to obtain argument fit.

After several trials, eventually a solution which works:
zzmf_data.frame(zzt=zz1$D[,1],zze=as.numeric(zz1$D[,1]<log(60)),zza=zz1$D[,3
],zza2=zz1$D[,3]^2)
zzsss_censorReg(censor(zzt,zze)~zza+zza2,distr="gaus",data=zzmf)
predict(zzsss,newdata=data.frame(zza=seq(5,10,.1),zza2=seq(5,10,.1)^2),p=.5,
conf=F,se=F)

The crucial point is that, in contrast to other versions of predict, such as
predict.lm or predict.gam, followind does not work:
S+>zzsss_censorReg(censor(zzt,zze)~zza+zza^2,distr="gaus",data=zzmf)
S+>predict(zzsss,newdata=data.frame(zza=seq(5,10,.1)),p=.5,conf=F,se=F)
Error in "[.data.frame"(X,  , i): undefined columns selected

In some way the names of the variables in newdata must match exactly the
terms in the original formula.
-----------------------
I shall be happy for all illuminating comments.
Dr. Luciano Molinari
Abt. EEG und AWE
Kinderspital Zürich
