1  7 [-0.195; -0.095) 4.363115 0.4730515
2 31  [-0.095; 0.005) 4.051821 0.5658295
3 14   [0.005; 0.105) 4.264527 0.9265701
4  6   [0.105; 0.205) 4.365794 1.2795916
5  2   [0.205; 0.238) 5.277778 0.3928371

============
By Sam Buttrey

resid <- residuals (fit.lm) # extract residuals
cut.points <- seq (.49, 2.51, by = .1) # or use seq (min (resid) - .1,
max(resid) + .1, by = .1) to ensure you get the whole range
cuts <- cut (resid, cut.points)
tapply (resid, cuts, mean) # produces vector of means.
out <- tapply (resid, cuts, function(x) c(length(x), mean(x), 
sqrt(var(x))))
# produces list(not matrix) with your results
matrix (unlist (out), ncol=3, byrow = T) # turn list into matrix

===========
by Don MacQueen

rsds <- residuals(fit.lm)
grps <- cut(rsds,breaks=seq(0.5,2.5,by=0.1)
tmp <- split(rsds,grps)

tmp.l <- lapply(tmp,FUN=length)
tmp.m <- lapply(tmp,FUN=mean)
tmp.v <- lapply(tmp,FUN=var)

df <- data.frame(grp=names(tmp.l),mean=tmp.l,
    var=tmp.v,std.dev=sqrt(tmp.v),n=tmp.l)

The cut() function is the one that splits the residuals up into the
desired intervals.
========
by Andy Liaw

Seems like the function cut() is what you need.

