I am a graduate student in economics.  My doctoral dissertation
needs a confidence interval on a quadratic form

x'Rx,  where R is a matrix of correlation coefficients
        (among portfolios).  It comes from data and is
        subject to random variation.
R is nxn matrix with n(n-1)/2 corrlations

My advisor, Prof. Vinod suggests finding confidence interval
for each element of R, using Euclidian matrix norm |R|,
randomly selecting among 2^m possible choices
associated with the lower and upper limit of the
interval for each distinct element among m=n(n-1)/2.
Choose the matrix with the largest value of |R|
as the upper limit and the one with the smallest value
of the norm |R| as the lower limit.

